    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T04:01:54Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T04:01:54Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iran's Provincial Population Distribution | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iran's provincial population patterns, census comparisons, urbanization differences, and the limits of historical regional data."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "reading-the-map",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A country of unequal population concentrations",
        "content": "A provincial population map answers a particular question: how many residents were counted inside each administrative territory? It does not directly show how crowded settlements are, how accessible services are, or how many people live in the provincial capital. Iran's census publications distinguish provincial population totals from the national population-density indicator and from urban and rural residence. Those distinctions should remain visible when interpreting regional differences. [s1]\n\nThe selected census results identify Tehran as the largest provincial population concentration in 2016, followed by Razavi Khorasan, Esfahan, Fars, and Khuzestan. The table below follows three contrasting provinces across the published census comparisons. It is a selection illustrating changes in national population shares, rather than a complete provincial ranking. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "provincial-shares",
        "type": "table",
        "content": {
          "title": "Selected provinces' shares of Iran's population at two censuses",
          "dataset_id": "selected-provincial-shares"
        },
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "regional-contrasts",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Population size and settlement pattern are different",
        "content": "The Stanford Iran 2040 study's analysis of the 2016 census emphasizes that provinces also differ in the balance between urban and rural residence. Qom, Tehran, Alborz, and Esfahan had comparatively small rural shares, whereas Sistan and Baluchestan, Golestan, and Hormozgan had comparatively large ones. A national urbanization figure therefore conceals quite different local settlement systems. [s2]\n\nThis matters when asking practical questions about population distribution. A provincial total alone cannot establish whether residents are concentrated in a metropolitan area or spread across smaller settlements. Likewise, a small share of the national population is not itself a measure of low population density: that requires an area denominator. Provincial comparisons should specify whether they concern residents, settlement categories, or people per unit of land. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "interpreting-change",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What a changing share can establish",
        "content": "Population share is a relative measure. A province can gain residents while losing national share if the country grows faster. Conversely, a rising share does not identify migration as the cause. The selected census tables document distribution; explaining the movement requires separate evidence on births, deaths, migration, and any administrative changes. [s1]\n\nThe historical comparisons are useful starting points for regional research, but they should not be relabeled as conditions in 2026. The Stanford study was published in 2017 and its geographical discussion principally interprets the preceding census. Its observations describe that historical baseline rather than an independently verified contemporary provincial distribution. [s2]\n\nThe most useful reading of the evidence is therefore layered: establish the census population, examine the urban–rural pattern, and investigate the mechanisms behind change. This preserves the difference between a documented geographical pattern and an explanation that still requires research. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
