  {
    "schema_version": "1.0",
    "page_id": "topic-10-1-6",
    "page_path": "/explore/society/demography/household-change/",
    "name": "Household Change",
    "level": 3,
    "parent_path": "/explore/society/demography/",
    "entity_type": "concept",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iran's census evidence records a long-term decline in average household size alongside changes in household composition. These changes involve childbearing, adult co-residence, marriage, and aging, and cannot be reduced to a single story about family life.",
    "tags": ["Households", "Family change", "Living arrangements", "Census", "Aging"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T04:01:54Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T04:01:54Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Household Change in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iran's shrinking average household size, persistent adult co-residence, changing living arrangements, and census evidence limitations."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "household-versus-family",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Size is only one part of household change",
        "content": "A household describes a living arrangement, while family relationships can extend beyond the people sharing a home. Research on Iran therefore examines both household size and composition: how many people live together, their relationships, and whether adult children or other relatives share the household. A smaller average does not by itself establish that extended-family ties have weakened. [s2]\n\nThe historical census series below documents a substantial decline in average household size. These are observations at particular censuses, with unequal gaps between them. They should not be read as annual measurements or as evidence that change followed a smooth path between census dates. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "household-size-table",
        "type": "table",
        "content": {
          "title": "Average household size at selected censuses",
          "dataset_id": "census-household-size"
        },
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "composition",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Fewer children did not mean fewer adults",
        "content": "Aghajanian and Thompson's analysis of census evidence through 2006 found that falling household size reflected fewer child members alongside persistent adult co-residence. Their findings challenge a simple assumption that fertility decline must quickly produce a uniform household consisting only of parents and dependent children. Unmarried adult children and extended households remained important parts of the picture. [s2]\n\nThe authors distinguish possible explanations from established causes. Continued co-residence might reflect preferences for extended-family living, constraints on independent housing, or a combination. Their analysis does not settle the balance between choice and economic necessity. This distinction matters: the same household structure can arise from different circumstances, and a census roster cannot fully explain the decisions behind it. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "aging-and-living-alone",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Aging adds another dimension",
        "content": "An author-repository abstract for a separate study of older Iranians reports different living arrangements for men and women. Using historical census evidence, the researchers found that older men commonly continued living with a spouse, while solitary living increased among older women and co-residence with married children declined. These findings concern the historical populations studied; they are not a current estimate of older people's living arrangements. [s3]\n\nThe abstract also identifies unanswered questions about older people's social, economic, and health circumstances. Living alone should therefore not automatically be interpreted as either independence or isolation. The household roster establishes who shares a home; assessing support and vulnerability requires additional evidence. Only the abstract was accessible for this source, so detailed findings from the full article are not claimed here. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-current-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Keeping different statistical sources separate",
        "content": "A later study using Iran's Household Income and Expenditure Survey also reports declining household size and changes in household headship. That survey-based evidence supports further investigation beyond the census baseline, but its observations have not been appended to the census dataset here. Survey estimates and census tabulations need a definition and weighting check before they can form a comparable series. [s4]\n\nThe defensible conclusion is that household change involves several dimensions at once: average size, the presence of children and adults, relationships between members, and living arrangements in old age. A contemporary account needs updated evidence on each dimension rather than a single average presented as a complete portrait of Iranian families. [s2][s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
