  {
    "schema_version": "1.0",
    "page_id": "topic-10-2",
    "page_path": "/explore/society/migration/",
    "name": "Migration",
    "level": 2,
    "parent_path": "/explore/society/",
    "entity_type": "concept",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Migration connected with Iran includes internal residential movement, international study and employment, emigration, and displacement across borders. These processes require different evidence because census migration counts, student enrollment, migrant populations, and return movements measure different things.",
    "tags": ["Migration", "Internal migration", "Student mobility", "Diaspora", "Displacement", "Afghan returnees"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T04:01:54Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T04:01:54Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Migration in and from Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iran's internal migration, international student mobility, diaspora questions, and Afghan returns while distinguishing incompatible measures."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "overview",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Several movements, several kinds of evidence",
        "content": "Migration is not a single stream out of Iran. The country's census evidence records movement within national borders, while international sources examine study abroad and movements between Iran and Afghanistan. These subjects overlap in people's lives, but the statistics describe different populations and events. An internal residential move, enrollment overseas, and a monitored border return cannot be combined into one migration total. [s1][s3][s5]\n\nThe distinction also matters for the questions readers ask. A provincial migration table can describe where people moved within Iran during a defined interval. Student statistics can identify participation in education abroad. Border monitoring can document returns and deportations. None, on its own, supplies a complete account of how many people have permanently left Iran or of the size of an Iranian diaspora. [s1][s3][s4][s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3", "s4", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "internal-movement",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Internal migration extends beyond village-to-city movement",
        "content": "Iran's historical urban transition makes rural-to-urban migration an important subject, but it is an incomplete description of more recent internal mobility. The KNOMAD report's background review, drawing on census research and UNFPA, identifies urban-to-urban movement as the dominant internal migration type by the later census intervals. A move between cities can cross a provincial boundary or remain within the same province. Settlement type and administrative boundary are separate classifications. [s2]\n\nThe Statistical Center's census summary distinguishes internal migrants moving between provinces from those moving within provinces. It also separates these categories from arrivals from abroad and an unknown category. These are observations for a census migration interval, not a count of every journey or a directly observed annual migration rate. [s1]\n\nThe UNFPA publication introducing its internal-migration study describes varied motives, including access to work and movement toward or away from urban settings. This range cautions against assuming that all moves have the same purpose or direction. The publication introduces a study focused on the historical census interval; its introductory description does not establish the prevalence of each motive today. [s6]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s6"]
      },
      {
        "id": "classification-diagram",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "Two separate ways to classify an internal move",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Internal residential move\"] -->|\"Classify administrative boundary\"| B[\"Within one province\"]\n  A -->|\"Classify administrative boundary\"| C[\"Between provinces\"]\n  A -->|\"Classify origin and destination settlements\"| D[\"Urban to urban\"]\n  A -->|\"Classify origin and destination settlements\"| E[\"Rural to urban\"]\n  A -->|\"Classify origin and destination settlements\"| F[\"Urban to rural\"]\n  A -->|\"Classify origin and destination settlements\"| G[\"Rural to rural\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The branches represent alternative classification dimensions, not additive groups. A move can belong to one administrative category and one settlement category simultaneously.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "study-work-diaspora",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Study, skilled migration, and diaspora are distinct questions",
        "content": "UNESCO defines international student mobility around physically crossing a national border for education in a destination different from the student's country of origin. Its guidance distinguishes this from simply holding foreign citizenship. Country of origin may be operationalized through prior education or residence, depending on available reporting. Consequently, a count of Iranian citizens at foreign universities and a count of internationally mobile students from Iran may not be identical. [s3]\n\nStudent enrollment abroad also does not establish permanent emigration. Measuring graduate retention or return requires evidence after study. Similarly, a defensible assessment of skilled migration needs explicit educational or occupational criteria and a defined period; an enrollment count cannot supply those missing dimensions. Diaspora research raises another boundary question: whether the population includes only people who migrated or also descendants. These questions remain research requirements here rather than grounds for an unsupported headline total. [s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "returns-and-protection",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Afghan returns require attention to coercion",
        "content": "UNHCR's border-monitoring report for 2025 documents returns from Iran that included deportations and departures under adverse circumstances. It describes restrictive policies, deteriorating conditions, and protection risks following return. The label 'return' therefore should not be read as proof that departure was freely chosen or that arrival in Afghanistan resolved protection needs. [s5]\n\nA subsequent response plan describes pressure on reception systems and communities receiving returnees, including demands on housing, services, and employment. Its planning expectations must remain separate from completed movements. Together, these documents show why migration reporting needs both a clearly defined measure and an account of the circumstances behind it. A border-flow report cannot substitute for a resident-population estimate, and an operational forecast cannot be presented as an observed outcome. [s4][s5][s7]",
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s5", "s7"]
      }
